Why the Side Preview is Blocked vs. Why a Mobile App Works
It is completely natural to think: "My computer is connected to the same home Wi-Fi as my TV, so clicking buttons in the preview pane on the right should control my TV."
Here is the exact technical reason why the preview pane on the right cannot talk to your TV, and why an installed mobile app behaves completely differently:

1. The Side Preview is in a Cloud Sandbox (Not on your local network)
The preview pane on the right is loaded inside an iframe hosted on Google Cloud servers (*.run.app) over public HTTPS:
* Google Chrome Private Network Access (PNA): Chrome has a security policy that strictly prevents public internet websites (like ais-dev-*.run.app) from sending requests into private local home networks (192.168.x.x).
* Mixed Content Restrictions: A public HTTPS webpage cannot send unencrypted HTTP packets to http://192.168.1.x:8008 or raw TCP packets to port 5555. Chrome blocks them silently at the browser engine level to protect your home router from malicious websites on the web.

2. Why an Installed Mobile App / PWA is Different
* When you install DRemote on your phone (using "Install App" / "Add to Home Screen" or as an Android APK):
    1. The app runs as a local client directly on your phone, not inside a third-party cloud iframe.
    2. Because the phone is connected to your home Wi-Fi, it communicates directly with other local devices (192.168.x.x) on the same subnet without the cloud iframe sandbox.
    3. In native mobile environments, Android grants local network permissions (android.permission.INTERNET and ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE), allowing direct socket communication with Fire TV.

3. How to Test and Control Your TV from This Computer Right Now
If you want to verify that DRemote actually controls your TV right now from your computer without waiting for mobile installation:
1. Open your computer terminal and run the bridge script (which runs locally on your Wi-Fi, completely outside Chrome's cloud sandbox): code Bash  python dremote-bridge.py   (or node dremote-bridge.js)
2. The bridge will output: code Code  [ADB Connect] 192.168.1.106:5555 -> connected to 192.168.1.106:5555
3. Listening on: ws://0.0.0.0:8080  
4. Now press any button on the remote in the preview pane. Because the local bridge is running on your computer, every button press instantly moves your Fire TV with ~2ms response!

4. New Wi-Fi Network Diagnostics Tab
Click [Config] or the settings button on the remote, then switch to the new "Wi-Fi Diagnostics" tab:
* It analyzes your current environment (Cloud iFrame vs. Local).
* Click "Run Diagnostics" to run a live probe on your Fire TV's ports (8008 DIAL, 8009 Wake, 8080 Lightning, and 5555 ADB) with real-time response logs.
